1
00:00:04,160 --> 00:00:05,930
Yeah, I miss you, too, sweetie.

2
00:00:05,990 --> 00:00:08,770
Listen, I got to go, but I'll see you tonight?

3
00:00:08,770 --> 00:00:11,020
Okay. Bye-bye. Bye-bye.

4
00:00:11,020 --> 00:00:13,850
No, you hang up first.

5
00:00:15,070 --> 00:00:17,070
Hello?

6
00:00:18,210 --> 00:00:20,560
Dude, I'm glad you finally got a girlfriend,

7
00:00:20,560 --> 00:00:22,600
but do you have to do all that lovey-dovey stuff

8
00:00:22,600 --> 00:00:23,970
in front of those of us who don't?

9
00:00:24,170 --> 00:00:25,540
Actually, he might have to.

10
00:00:25,610 --> 00:00:28,340
There's an economic concept known as a positional good

11
00:00:28,410 --> 00:00:31,070
in which an object is only valued by the possessor

12
00:00:31,140 --> 00:00:32,770
because it's not possessed by others.

13
00:00:32,840 --> 00:00:36,510
The term was coined in 1976 by economist Fred Hirsch

14
00:00:36,570 --> 00:00:40,610
to replace the more colloquial, but less precise "neener-neener."

15
00:00:40,670 --> 00:00:41,770
That's not true.

16
00:00:41,840 --> 00:00:43,170
My happiness is not dependent

17
00:00:43,240 --> 00:00:45,640
on my best friend being miserable and alone.

18
00:00:45,710 --> 00:00:46,710
Thank you.

19
00:00:46,770 --> 00:00:49,640
Although, I'd be lying if I said it wasn't a little bit of a perk.

20
00:00:49,710 --> 00:00:51,720
Who's miserable and alone?

21
00:00:51,720 --> 00:00:52,340
Me.

22
00:00:52,970 --> 00:00:55,550
I used to be like that. Then I got a girlfriend.

23
00:00:55,550 --> 00:01:00,170
In pre-1976 terms, neener-neener.

24
00:01:00,640 --> 00:01:03,750
Hey, what are you and Bernadette doing for your first Valentine's Day?

25
00:01:03,750 --> 00:01:05,560
Yeah, I am pulling out all the stops.

26
00:01:05,560 --> 00:01:09,690
There's a $39.95 lover's special at P.F. Chang's.

27
00:01:10,360 --> 00:01:11,520
Egg rolls, dumplings,

28
00:01:11,590 --> 00:01:13,890
bottomless wok, and you get your picture taken

29
00:01:13,960 --> 00:01:16,720
on the big marble horse out front.

30
00:01:16,790 --> 00:01:20,420
Given that Saint Valentine was a third century Roman priest

31
00:01:20,490 --> 00:01:22,120
who was stoned and beheaded,

32
00:01:22,190 --> 00:01:24,620
wouldn't a more appropriate celebration of the evening

33
00:01:24,690 --> 00:01:27,800
be taking one's steady gal to witness a brutal murder?

34
00:01:28,320 --> 00:01:31,740
I understand your point, but given a choice,

35
00:01:31,740 --> 00:01:34,000
Jews always go with Chinese food.

36
00:01:35,760 --> 00:01:36,890
Well, if anyone's interested,

37
00:01:36,960 --> 00:01:38,360
I'll be spending this Valentine's Day

38
00:01:38,420 --> 00:01:40,390
the same way I spend every Valentine's Day.

39
00:01:40,460 --> 00:01:42,220
Buying a rotisserie chicken from the supermarket,

40
00:01:42,290 --> 00:01:44,060
taking it home, standing over the sink

41
00:01:44,120 --> 00:01:47,890
and eating it out of the package with my bare hands like an animal.

42
00:01:47,960 --> 00:01:51,890
Okay, so to sum up: one giant marble horse,

43
00:01:51,960 --> 00:01:56,320
one asinine comment, one lonely man and his chicken.

44
00:01:56,390 --> 00:01:58,330
And let's see. Who's left?

45
00:01:58,330 --> 00:02:00,620
Oh, that's right. My plans.

46
00:02:04,060 --> 00:02:05,820
Isn't anyone going to ask?

47
00:02:05,820 --> 00:02:07,550
Fine, tell us you're going to have sex with Penny.

48
00:02:07,620 --> 00:02:09,250
That's not what I was going to tell you.

49
00:02:09,320 --> 00:02:12,550
It's okay. I don't mind hearing about your sex life.

50
00:02:12,620 --> 00:02:13,990
It's his that bugs me.

51
00:02:15,890 --> 00:02:18,690
Guess who the university is sending to Switzerland

52
00:02:18,750 --> 00:02:21,190
to attend a conference and see the CERN supercollider

53
00:02:21,250 --> 00:02:23,020
on February 14?

54
00:02:23,090 --> 00:02:25,120
Professor Norton, although, God knows why.

55
00:02:25,190 --> 00:02:26,690
He hasn't published anything of note

56
00:02:26,750 --> 00:02:28,620
since he won that Nobel Prize.

57
00:02:28,690 --> 00:02:30,290
Actually, Professor Norton can't make it.

58
00:02:30,350 --> 00:02:31,790
He threw his back out rock climbing.

59
00:02:31,850 --> 00:02:34,650
I heard he threw his back out climbing on his new girlfriend.

60
00:02:34,720 --> 00:02:36,390
The big-boobed weather girl on Channel 2?

61
00:02:36,450 --> 00:02:37,120
That's the one.

62
00:02:37,190 --> 00:02:40,720
In any case, they're asking me to fill in for him.

63
00:02:40,790 --> 00:02:43,620
In Switzerland or with the big-boobed weather girl?

64
00:02:43,690 --> 00:02:45,450
Switzerland.

65
00:02:45,520 --> 00:02:47,450
And I get to bring a guest!

66
00:02:47,520 --> 00:02:49,320
Oh, man! No way, dude!

67
00:02:49,390 --> 00:02:50,490
This is incredible!

68
00:02:50,550 --> 00:02:51,990
I'm so happy,

69
00:02:52,050 --> 00:02:54,690
I'm not even going to question their judgment in picking you.

70
00:02:54,750 --> 00:02:57,490
I'm just going to run home and start packing.

71
00:02:59,390 --> 00:03:02,390
Why wouldn't you take Penny?

72
00:03:02,450 --> 00:03:06,050
I am taking Penny.

73
00:03:07,890 --> 00:03:11,520
Well, then I anticipate an awkward situation when you get home.

74
00:03:39,800 --> 00:03:41,100
Sheldon, you got a minute?

75
00:03:41,170 --> 00:03:42,800
Of course, my good friend.

76
00:03:42,870 --> 00:03:44,000
Come in.

77
00:03:44,070 --> 00:03:46,440
Help me out.

78
00:03:46,500 --> 00:03:49,770
Which ski hat says aprey supercollider?

79
00:03:52,100 --> 00:03:54,740
Sheldon, I'm not taking you to Switzerland.

80
00:03:54,800 --> 00:03:56,240
Well, of course you are.

81
00:03:56,300 --> 00:03:57,600
Who else would you take?

82
00:03:57,670 --> 00:03:58,740
Penny.

83
00:03:58,800 --> 00:04:00,870
What? That's absurd.

84
00:04:00,870 --> 00:04:04,270
Penny has no interest in subatomic particle research.

85
00:04:04,340 --> 00:04:07,070
Yes, but it'll be Valentine's Day.

86
00:04:07,140 --> 00:04:09,270
We can go sightseeing and skiing

87
00:04:09,340 --> 00:04:11,640
and make love in front of a roaring fire

88
00:04:11,700 --> 00:04:14,270
in the shadow of the snowcapped Alps.

89
00:04:14,340 --> 00:04:19,100
But Penny has no interest in subatomic particle research.

90
00:04:19,170 --> 00:04:21,400
I'm sorry, Sheldon.

91
00:04:21,470 --> 00:04:22,440
Sorry?

92
00:04:22,500 --> 00:04:25,500
I've been dreaming about going to the Large Hadron Collider

93
00:04:25,570 --> 00:04:27,140
since I was nine years old.

94
00:04:27,200 --> 00:04:29,500
Yeah, well, I've been dreaming about spending Valentine's Day

95
00:04:29,570 --> 00:04:32,170
with a girl since I was six.

96
00:04:32,240 --> 00:04:34,400
Shame on you!

97
00:04:34,470 --> 00:04:37,970
That's no dream for a scientist!

98
00:04:39,340 --> 00:04:41,140
Okay, what's the big surprise?

99
00:04:41,200 --> 00:04:42,800
Just a minute.

100
00:04:42,870 --> 00:04:45,740
This tray contains clues

101
00:04:45,800 --> 00:04:49,370
as to what you and I are going to be doing on Valentine's Day.

102
00:04:49,440 --> 00:04:51,670
Oh. Wow. Okay.

103
00:04:51,740 --> 00:04:53,000
Let's see.

104
00:04:53,070 --> 00:04:55,240
We've got, uh, milk chocolate,

105
00:04:55,340 --> 00:04:58,670
Swiss cheese, fondue.

106
00:04:58,940 --> 00:05:02,250
My lactose-intolerant boyfriend is going to eat all this.

107
00:05:02,250 --> 00:05:06,040
Then I'm going to climb on his back and rocket to the moon?

108
00:05:09,740 --> 00:05:11,540
No.

109
00:05:12,700 --> 00:05:15,170
But it does involve air travel.

110
00:05:16,140 --> 00:05:18,000
Okay, um...

111
00:05:18,040 --> 00:05:24,240
let me slice this Swiss cheese with my Swiss army knife,

112
00:05:24,300 --> 00:05:30,840
and then you can wash it down with a cup of Swiss Miss instant cocoa.

113
00:05:30,900 --> 00:05:33,970
Okay, I'm starting to think Swiss is key here.

114
00:05:34,040 --> 00:05:36,140
Uh-huh.

115
00:05:36,200 --> 00:05:38,870
We're going to Disneyland and ride the Matterhorn?

116
00:05:41,570 --> 00:05:43,500
How does that involve air travel?

117
00:05:43,570 --> 00:05:48,470
We're going to Disney World and ride the Matterhorn?

118
00:05:48,540 --> 00:05:50,040
No.

119
00:05:50,100 --> 00:05:53,170
Okay, sweetie, this started out fun, but I'm over it.

120
00:05:53,240 --> 00:05:56,040
We're going to Switzerland

121
00:05:56,100 --> 00:05:58,370
to see the CERN supercollider!

122
00:06:00,300 --> 00:06:02,740
And ski. We'll also go skiing.

123
00:06:02,800 --> 00:06:05,340
We're going skiing in Switzerland?!

124
00:06:05,400 --> 00:06:07,400
Well, you'll ski, I'll fall, but, yeah,

125
00:06:07,470 --> 00:06:10,440
we will be in Switzerland for Valentine's Day.

126
00:06:10,500 --> 00:06:13,450
Oh, my God, Leonard! That's incredible!

127
00:06:13,540 --> 00:06:15,450
Not so fast.

128
00:06:15,450 --> 00:06:18,750
You might want to hold off on lighting your rabeliechtli, Penny.

129
00:06:18,810 --> 00:06:20,310
Rabe-what-ly?

130
00:06:20,380 --> 00:06:22,980
Rabeliechtli. It means turnip light

131
00:06:23,050 --> 00:06:26,050
and refers to a traditional lantern hand-carved from a root vegetable

132
00:06:26,110 --> 00:06:28,050
and used to celebrate certain Swiss festivals.

133
00:06:28,110 --> 00:06:29,610
Which you will not be celebrating

134
00:06:29,680 --> 00:06:32,580
because A: these festivals occur in the fall;

135
00:06:32,650 --> 00:06:34,050
and B:

136
00:06:34,110 --> 00:06:36,750
you will not be going to Switzerland.

137
00:06:36,810 --> 00:06:38,380
Sheldon, we've been through this.

138
00:06:38,450 --> 00:06:39,910
I'm taking Penny.

139
00:06:39,980 --> 00:06:42,250
Afraid not. Do you recognize this?

140
00:06:42,310 --> 00:06:44,250
Not the roommate agreement.

141
00:06:44,310 --> 00:06:46,150
Indeed, the roommate agreement.

142
00:06:46,210 --> 00:06:48,950
I call your attention to the Friendship Rider

143
00:06:49,010 --> 00:06:50,780
in Appendix C: Future Commitments.

144
00:06:50,850 --> 00:06:53,780
"Number 37: in the event one friend is ever invited

145
00:06:53,850 --> 00:06:55,850
"to visit the Large Hadron Collider,

146
00:06:55,910 --> 00:06:57,510
"now under construction in Switzerland,

147
00:06:57,580 --> 00:06:59,980
he shall invite the other friend to accompany him."

148
00:07:00,050 --> 00:07:01,580
Oh, for God's sakes.

149
00:07:01,650 --> 00:07:04,310
You actually put that in an agreement?

150
00:07:04,380 --> 00:07:06,180
Yeah. We also put in what happens

151
00:07:06,250 --> 00:07:08,110
if one of us wins a MacArthur Grant,

152
00:07:08,180 --> 00:07:09,850
or if one of us gets superpowers,

153
00:07:09,910 --> 00:07:12,450
or if one of us is bitten by a zombie.

154
00:07:12,510 --> 00:07:15,650
He can't kill me, even if I turn.

155
00:07:15,710 --> 00:07:19,450
Is there anything in there about if one of you gets a girlfriend?

156
00:07:19,510 --> 00:07:21,950
No, that seemed a little farfetched.

157
00:07:24,610 --> 00:07:27,680
Sheldon, do you really expect to enforce this?

158
00:07:27,750 --> 00:07:28,880
I've lived up

159
00:07:28,950 --> 00:07:30,550
to all my commitments under the agreement.

160
00:07:30,610 --> 00:07:32,950
At least once a day I ask how you are,

161
00:07:33,010 --> 00:07:34,410
even though I simply don't care.

162
00:07:34,480 --> 00:07:35,780
I no longer

163
00:07:35,850 --> 00:07:38,880
stage spontaneous biohazard drills after 10:00 p.m.

164
00:07:38,950 --> 00:07:42,510
And I abandoned my goal to master Tuvan throat singing.

165
00:07:42,580 --> 00:07:44,780
- Okay, I know I shouldn't ask, but what is...? - No.

166
00:07:53,350 --> 00:07:56,780
I'd be much further along if I'd been allowed to practice.

167
00:07:56,850 --> 00:07:59,710
Look, Sheldon, i know it's in the agreement,

168
00:07:59,780 --> 00:08:03,050
and if you turn into a zombie, I promise I will not kill you.

169
00:08:03,110 --> 00:08:05,180
In fact, I'll even let you eat my brains.

170
00:08:05,250 --> 00:08:07,280
But I am taking Penny to Switzerland.

171
00:08:07,350 --> 00:08:08,780
Is that your final decision?

172
00:08:08,850 --> 00:08:09,780
It is.

173
00:08:09,850 --> 00:08:12,750
Very well.

174
00:08:14,500 --> 00:08:16,530
It's not over, is it?

175
00:08:16,630 --> 00:08:18,630
What do you think?

176
00:08:32,000 --> 00:08:35,040
Got a bit of traffic this morning, huh?

177
00:08:37,600 --> 00:08:40,300
Think it's gonna rain?

178
00:08:42,000 --> 00:08:43,200
Instead of underpants,

179
00:08:43,200 --> 00:08:45,500
I covered my crotch with potato salad this morning.

180
00:08:45,500 --> 00:08:47,330
Thoughts?

181
00:08:48,500 --> 00:08:50,000
Okay, I know what'll cheer you up.

182
00:08:50,000 --> 00:08:53,000
Let's play one of your driving games.

183
00:08:53,100 --> 00:08:55,100
All right.

184
00:08:55,100 --> 00:08:56,740
This game is called Traitors.

185
00:08:58,700 --> 00:09:00,600
I will name three historical figures,

186
00:09:00,670 --> 00:09:01,940
you put them in order

187
00:09:02,000 --> 00:09:03,740
of the heinousness of their betrayal.

188
00:09:03,800 --> 00:09:08,470
Benedict Arnold, Judas, Dr. Leonard Hofstadter.

189
00:09:08,540 --> 00:09:11,970
You really think I belong with Benedict Arnold and Judas?

190
00:09:12,040 --> 00:09:13,140
You're right.

191
00:09:13,200 --> 00:09:16,100
Judas had the decency to hang himself after what he did.

192
00:09:16,170 --> 00:09:19,980
Come on, Sheldon. Can't you at least try to understand

193
00:09:19,980 --> 00:09:21,070
how much this means to me?

194
00:09:22,300 --> 00:09:24,740
Round two: Leonard Hofstadter,

195
00:09:24,800 --> 00:09:27,840
Darth Vader, Rupert Murdoch.

196
00:09:27,900 --> 00:09:29,140
Rupert Murdoch?

197
00:09:29,200 --> 00:09:32,340
He owns Fox, and they cancelled Firefly.

198
00:09:33,870 --> 00:09:38,040
Hint: he and Darth Vader are tied for #2.

199
00:09:41,700 --> 00:09:43,840
I'm sorry I couldn't hang with you last night.

200
00:09:43,900 --> 00:09:45,300
I had a date with Bernadette.

201
00:09:45,370 --> 00:09:46,500
I know.

202
00:09:46,570 --> 00:09:48,440
I saw the Tweet.

203
00:09:48,500 --> 00:09:50,000
So, what did you end up doing?

204
00:09:50,070 --> 00:09:51,170
Not much.

205
00:09:51,240 --> 00:09:52,640
Nuked a burrito. Oh.

206
00:09:52,700 --> 00:09:55,170
Prayed to the Hindu god Urvashi that your bowels would loosen

207
00:09:55,240 --> 00:09:57,300
and your penis would droop like a willow tree.

208
00:09:58,940 --> 00:10:00,240
You seen Sheldon?

209
00:10:00,300 --> 00:10:03,060
No. Is he still mad about the supercollider?

210
00:10:03,060 --> 00:10:05,200
Yeah. He thinks I betrayed him.

211
00:10:05,200 --> 00:10:07,570
I mean, come on. What would you guys do if you were me?

212
00:10:07,640 --> 00:10:10,000
I'd take Sheldon to Switzerland.

213
00:10:10,070 --> 00:10:11,170
Seriously?

214
00:10:11,240 --> 00:10:12,340
Absolutely.

215
00:10:12,400 --> 00:10:13,570
And I'd leave him there.

216
00:10:20,940 --> 00:10:23,900
What the hell is that?

217
00:10:23,970 --> 00:10:25,940
Uh, let's see.

218
00:10:25,940 --> 00:10:28,540
30 pieces of silverware.

219
00:10:34,300 --> 00:10:37,300
Grieg "Peer Gynt - Morning"

220
00:10:50,900 --> 00:10:52,500
Morning, old chum.

221
00:10:52,660 --> 00:10:54,000
What's going on?

222
00:10:54,060 --> 00:10:55,830
I've made you breakfast.

223
00:10:55,900 --> 00:10:57,860
Juice, coffee,

224
00:10:57,930 --> 00:10:59,860
and pancakes in the shape of some

225
00:10:59,930 --> 00:11:01,760
of your favorite fictional characters.

226
00:11:01,830 --> 00:11:03,800
See, here's Frodo.

227
00:11:03,860 --> 00:11:05,960
You made Frodo pancakes?

228
00:11:06,030 --> 00:11:09,030
Yeah, I used coconut shavings to do the hair on his feet.

229
00:11:09,100 --> 00:11:12,460
If you need to void your bladder before eating,

230
00:11:12,530 --> 00:11:14,900
I can keep them warm with this beret

231
00:11:14,960 --> 00:11:16,000
that I thoroughly laundered

232
00:11:16,060 --> 00:11:17,560
and pressed into service as a pancake cozy.

233
00:11:18,600 --> 00:11:21,100
Why are you doing this?

234
00:11:21,160 --> 00:11:24,230
It's by way of an apology for my recent behavior.

235
00:11:24,300 --> 00:11:28,330
I've had some time to reflect and I've come to realize

236
00:11:28,400 --> 00:11:31,600
that friendship is not an aggregation of written agreements.

237
00:11:31,660 --> 00:11:35,430
It's a result of two people respecting and caring for each other.

238
00:11:35,500 --> 00:11:37,000
Butterscotch scone?

239
00:11:37,060 --> 00:11:39,000
Thanks.

240
00:11:41,330 --> 00:11:42,560
It's good.

241
00:11:42,630 --> 00:11:45,460
What you're tasting is respect and affection.

242
00:11:46,660 --> 00:11:49,100
And about a pound of Crisco.

243
00:11:50,060 --> 00:11:52,900
After you've finished breakfast, i thought we could spend the day

244
00:11:52,960 --> 00:11:56,660
watching the final season of Babylon 5 with director commentary.

245
00:11:56,730 --> 00:11:58,330
You hate Babylon 5.

246
00:11:58,400 --> 00:12:00,760
I do. It fails as drama/science fiction,

247
00:12:00,830 --> 00:12:02,800
and it's hopelessly derivative.

248
00:12:02,860 --> 00:12:05,760
But you like it, and you're my friend.

249
00:12:05,830 --> 00:12:07,900
Okay, great.

250
00:12:10,060 --> 00:12:11,930
Still not taking you to Switzerland.

251
00:12:12,000 --> 00:12:13,700
Drat.

252
00:12:17,500 --> 00:12:19,660
No Frodo for you.

253
00:12:23,000 --> 00:12:24,840
Hello.

254
00:12:24,900 --> 00:12:26,240
Hello.

255
00:12:26,300 --> 00:12:28,200
All right, let's dispense with the friendly banter.

256
00:12:29,500 --> 00:12:31,040
I believe you know why I'm here.

257
00:12:31,100 --> 00:12:33,540
Well, I always figured it was to study us,

258
00:12:33,600 --> 00:12:34,700
discover our weaknesses,

259
00:12:34,770 --> 00:12:37,870
and report back to your alien overlords.

260
00:12:37,200 --> 00:12:39,170
Yes, amusing.

261
00:12:39,230 --> 00:12:41,630
Extraordinary intelligence

262
00:12:41,700 --> 00:12:44,430
might well appear extraterrestrial to you,

263
00:12:44,500 --> 00:12:46,700
but let me be more specific.

264
00:12:46,770 --> 00:12:49,270
I believe you know why I'm here in the laundry room.

265
00:12:50,000 --> 00:12:52,640
Better acoustics for your throat singing?

266
00:13:00,270 --> 00:13:02,240
It's actually not bad.

267
00:13:03,900 --> 00:13:06,570
But my true purpose in being here

268
00:13:06,640 --> 00:13:10,440
will be revealed in this brief PowerPoint presentation.

269
00:13:10,500 --> 00:13:12,040
Lights.

270
00:13:21,240 --> 00:13:24,000
Why Sheldon Cooper, PhD, should go to Switzerland

271
00:13:24,070 --> 00:13:25,670
to see the CERN supercollider:

272
00:13:25,740 --> 00:13:28,040
a PowerPoint presentation by Sheldon Cooper, PhD.

273
00:13:29,070 --> 00:13:31,970
Oh, for God's sake.

274
00:13:32,040 --> 00:13:34,100
Here we have a highly gifted researcher

275
00:13:34,700 --> 00:13:36,170
in the field of particle physics

276
00:13:36,240 --> 00:13:38,170
whose work has brought him to the precipice

277
00:13:38,240 --> 00:13:40,770
of forever changing mankind's understanding of the universe.

278
00:13:40,840 --> 00:13:42,340
AKA me.

279
00:13:42,400 --> 00:13:45,370
And here we have a waitress

280
00:13:45,440 --> 00:13:47,770
brushing her teeth with her finger.

281
00:13:49,000 --> 00:13:51,000
AKA you.

282
00:13:51,070 --> 00:13:52,200
I'm sorry.

283
00:13:52,270 --> 00:13:53,670
Is this supposed to be buttering me up?

284
00:13:53,740 --> 00:13:56,770
Please hold all questions till the end of the presentation.

285
00:13:56,840 --> 00:13:59,440
This is the Large Hadron Collider

286
00:13:59,500 --> 00:14:01,000
at CERN in Switzerland,

287
00:14:01,070 --> 00:14:03,740
the product of decades of planning and construction.

288
00:14:03,800 --> 00:14:06,670
It is a Mecca for physicists the world over.

289
00:14:06,740 --> 00:14:09,200
This is Bath and Body Works

290
00:14:09,270 --> 00:14:11,240
on Colorado Boulevard.

291
00:14:11,300 --> 00:14:13,240
They sell scented soaps and lotions,

292
00:14:13,300 --> 00:14:15,240
some of which contain glitter.

293
00:14:15,300 --> 00:14:18,600
Now, let's see if we can match the individual

294
00:14:18,670 --> 00:14:20,640
to the appropriate destination.

295
00:14:20,700 --> 00:14:22,000
O-kay.

296
00:14:22,070 --> 00:14:23,200
Show's over.

297
00:14:23,270 --> 00:14:24,640
No, it's not.

298
00:14:24,700 --> 00:14:25,800
I've got five more slides.

299
00:14:25,870 --> 00:14:28,300
Sheldon, this is Leonard's decision.

300
00:14:28,370 --> 00:14:30,740
He invited me to Switzerland, and I intend to go.

301
00:14:34,000 --> 00:14:36,930
Very well.

302
00:14:37,000 --> 00:14:38,930
Enjoy yourself.

303
00:14:39,000 --> 00:14:40,800
You're going to be in the presence of something

304
00:14:40,870 --> 00:14:42,470
that I've dreamed of seeing for decades.

305
00:14:43,600 --> 00:14:45,800
I just hope you'll be able to appreciate

306
00:14:45,800 --> 00:14:49,700
the magnitude of where you are and what it represents.

307
00:14:51,740 --> 00:14:54,240
I'll talk to Leonard.

308
00:14:54,250 --> 00:14:56,400
You will?

309
00:14:56,400 --> 00:14:57,000
Yes.

310
00:14:57,800 --> 00:15:00,970
If it means that much to you, you should go.

311
00:15:01,040 --> 00:15:04,040
Oh, Penny, thank you!

312
00:15:05,870 --> 00:15:07,200
You're welcome.

313
00:15:07,200 --> 00:15:08,000
Since I rarely hug,

314
00:15:08,000 --> 00:15:12,400
I'm relying on your expertise regarding duration.

315
00:15:12,470 --> 00:15:13,940
I think we're there.

316
00:15:14,000 --> 00:15:15,840
Oh, good.

317
00:15:15,900 --> 00:15:17,770
Bye-bye.

318
00:15:19,900 --> 00:15:22,200
You know what?

319
00:15:22,270 --> 00:15:23,700
Even though I don't have a girlfriend,

320
00:15:23,770 --> 00:15:25,690
I can still have a good time on Valentine's Day.

321
00:15:25,690 --> 00:15:28,370
Trust me, you can't. I've tried.

322
00:15:28,430 --> 00:15:31,200
No, no, no, I'm going to have a "me" day.

323
00:15:31,270 --> 00:15:33,930
First I'm going to go to one of those SPAs in Koreatown,

324
00:15:34,000 --> 00:15:35,700
take a steam and get a massage.

325
00:15:35,770 --> 00:15:39,900
Then I'm going to stop at a pet store and get licked by puppies.

326
00:15:42,100 --> 00:15:43,770
Sheldon, I need to talk to you.

327
00:15:43,830 --> 00:15:47,370
All right, my friend. Would you like some Cholermus?

328
00:15:47,430 --> 00:15:48,370
Some what?

329
00:15:48,370 --> 00:15:51,060
Cholermus. It's a traditional Swiss breakfast dish.

330
00:15:51,060 --> 00:15:53,260
I'm preparing my gastrointestinal system

331
00:15:53,320 --> 00:15:55,290
for the exotic cuisine of Switzerland.

332
00:15:55,360 --> 00:15:58,720
You're not going to Switzerland!

333
00:15:58,790 --> 00:16:00,190
Oh, but I am.

334
00:16:00,260 --> 00:16:01,990
Didn't Penny tell you the good news?

335
00:16:02,060 --> 00:16:03,660
She told me that you went behind my back

336
00:16:03,720 --> 00:16:05,690
to guilt her into letting you go instead of her.

337
00:16:05,760 --> 00:16:07,920
Yes, that good news.

338
00:16:09,090 --> 00:16:10,860
Well, forget it. I was the one who was invited.

339
00:16:10,920 --> 00:16:12,390
I get to decide who goes with me,

340
00:16:12,460 --> 00:16:14,060
and it's Penny, not you!

341
00:16:14,120 --> 00:16:15,890
Howard, could you lower the lights?

342
00:16:15,960 --> 00:16:17,890
I have a short PowerPoint presentation.

343
00:16:17,960 --> 00:16:20,290
I don't need to see your presentation.

344
00:16:20,360 --> 00:16:22,190
This discussion is over!

345
00:16:22,260 --> 00:16:24,190
That's a somewhat ambiguous response.

346
00:16:24,260 --> 00:16:25,820
Am I going or not?

347
00:16:25,890 --> 00:16:28,160
Sheldon, at this point,

348
00:16:28,220 --> 00:16:30,590
I would go by myself before I would take you.

349
00:16:30,660 --> 00:16:31,660
Really?

350
00:16:31,720 --> 00:16:33,020
Yeah, really.

351
00:16:33,090 --> 00:16:36,190
Well, then, you leave me no alternative.

352
00:16:36,260 --> 00:16:37,890
From this moment forward,

353
00:16:37,960 --> 00:16:41,060
we can be roommates, but we will no longer be friends.

354
00:16:41,120 --> 00:16:43,120
I'm sorry you feel that way.

355
00:16:43,190 --> 00:16:44,720
I don't think you're fully aware

356
00:16:44,790 --> 00:16:47,060
of the ramifications here, Leonard.

357
00:16:47,120 --> 00:16:49,520
Why don't you enlighten me?

358
00:16:49,590 --> 00:16:52,190
With the friendship clause of our Roommate Agreement nullified

359
00:16:52,260 --> 00:16:54,460
you are no longer entitled to accompany me

360
00:16:54,520 --> 00:16:56,920
to go swimming at Bill Gates' house should I be invited.

361
00:17:01,590 --> 00:17:04,060
Ooh, that's gotta sting.

362
00:17:07,820 --> 00:17:09,420
Just think, this time tomorrow

363
00:17:09,490 --> 00:17:12,760
we'll be in Geneva, Switzerland for our first Valentine's Day.

364
00:17:12,820 --> 00:17:14,260
I know!

365
00:17:14,320 --> 00:17:15,590
I went shopping today

366
00:17:15,660 --> 00:17:17,920
and bought special undies for the occasion.

367
00:17:17,990 --> 00:17:21,190
Thermal?'Cause it's gonna be cold.

368
00:17:21,260 --> 00:17:24,190
Think it through, Leonard.

369
00:17:28,090 --> 00:17:30,020
Gesundheit.

370
00:17:30,090 --> 00:17:32,520
Or as they say in Switzerland,

371
00:17:32,590 --> 00:17:35,020
Gesundheit.

372
00:17:35,090 --> 00:17:37,060
You getting a cold?

373
00:17:37,120 --> 00:17:39,160
No, no, it's probably just allergies.

374
00:17:40,920 --> 00:17:42,690
Do you want an allergy pill?

375
00:17:42,760 --> 00:17:43,920
'Cause I have 'em all.

376
00:17:43,990 --> 00:17:45,490
Prescription, nonprescription,

377
00:17:45,560 --> 00:17:47,520
foreign, domestic, experimental.

378
00:17:47,590 --> 00:17:49,290
Do any of them work?

379
00:17:49,360 --> 00:17:52,360
Not really, I'm just an enthusiast.

380
00:18:02,900 --> 00:18:04,040
Penny?

381
00:18:06,100 --> 00:18:07,870
You okay?

382
00:18:07,940 --> 00:18:09,940
Did that sound okay to you?

383
00:18:11,400 --> 00:18:14,370
Do not come in here!

384
00:18:14,440 --> 00:18:15,870
What's going on?

385
00:18:15,940 --> 00:18:17,200
I'm having a tea party.

386
00:18:17,270 --> 00:18:18,540
What do you think's going on?

387
00:18:21,640 --> 00:18:24,440
I think I might have the flu.

388
00:18:26,470 --> 00:18:28,600
Or the plague.

389
00:18:30,870 --> 00:18:32,670
Well, our plane leaves at 9:00 a.m.

390
00:18:32,740 --> 00:18:34,670
Do you think you'll feel better by then?

391
00:18:36,740 --> 00:18:38,200
Yep.

392
00:18:38,270 --> 00:18:40,240
'Cause I'm gonna be dead.

393
00:18:43,840 --> 00:18:45,770
Hey, Sheldon?

394
00:18:45,840 --> 00:18:47,600
Listen, Penny is pretty sick

395
00:18:47,670 --> 00:18:49,870
and she's not going to be able to go to Switzerland.

396
00:18:49,940 --> 00:18:53,670
So if you're still interested, you're welcome to come.

397
00:19:01,240 --> 00:19:02,870
Great.

398
00:19:02,940 --> 00:19:04,140
I'll start packing.

399
00:19:06,170 --> 00:19:08,840
In a minute.

400
00:19:11,740 --> 00:19:14,700
Oh, look, it's the Cholermus.

401
00:19:27,000 --> 00:19:28,600
Here's your soup.

402
00:19:28,670 --> 00:19:30,200
- Chicken? - Yes.

403
00:19:30,270 --> 00:19:32,170
- With the little stars? - Yes.

404
00:19:32,230 --> 00:19:33,770
Heated to 180 degrees?

405
00:19:33,830 --> 00:19:37,830
Why don't I pour it in your lap and you can tell me?

406
00:19:38,870 --> 00:19:40,300
You don't have to be mean.

407
00:19:40,370 --> 00:19:41,500
I'm sick.

408
00:19:41,570 --> 00:19:43,400
Yeah, well, I'm sick, too.

409
00:19:43,470 --> 00:19:45,300
Not my problem.

410
00:19:46,570 --> 00:19:48,930
I just don't understand how this happened to me.

411
00:19:49,000 --> 00:19:51,270
I'm scrupulous about my hygiene.

412
00:19:51,330 --> 00:19:53,570
I regularly disinfect my hands,

413
00:19:53,630 --> 00:19:57,600
and I avoid contact with other people on general principle.

414
00:19:57,670 --> 00:20:00,070
I don't know what to tell you, Sheldon.

415
00:20:02,300 --> 00:20:05,200
Oh, Penny, thank you!

416
00:20:05,270 --> 00:20:08,400
You're welcome.

417
00:20:14,230 --> 00:20:15,970
It's you!

418
00:20:16,030 --> 00:20:17,900
I touched you!

419
00:20:17,970 --> 00:20:20,570
Happy Valentine's Day.

420
00:20:30,530 --> 00:20:33,330
Oh, my goodness, look at this room!

421
00:20:33,400 --> 00:20:35,330
Champagne!

422
00:20:35,400 --> 00:20:37,330
Roses!

423
00:20:37,400 --> 00:20:40,370
And little chocolates!

424
00:20:40,430 --> 00:20:43,730
This is going to be the best Valentine's Day ever.

425
00:20:44,730 --> 00:20:47,900
Yeah, I forgot about all this.

426
00:20:47,970 --> 00:20:49,870
But I never will.

